WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.243
Go's static types describe what code
expects after values have entered the program.

2
00:00:04.323 --> 00:00:07.327
They do not prove that runtime data is
valid, complete, authorized,

3
00:00:07.407 --> 00:00:08.936
or safe for the current operation.

4
00:00:09.016 --> 00:00:11.652
HTTP request bodies, query parameters,
headers, cookies,

5
00:00:11.732 --> 00:00:14.631
environment variables, files, queue
events, database records,

6
00:00:14.711 --> 00:00:18.454
and third-party API responses should all
be treated as untrusted at their boundary.

7
00:00:18.534 --> 00:00:21.064
Validation is more than checking whether a
field exists.

8
00:00:21.144 --> 00:00:24.570
A good boundary checks shape, size, range,
allowed values, required fields,

9
00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:27.181
encoding assumptions, and consistency
between fields.

10
00:00:27.261 --> 00:00:30.845
For example, an identifier may have the
right type but still refer to an object the

11
00:00:30.925 --> 00:00:32.085
caller should not access.

12
00:00:32.165 --> 00:00:35.222
A timestamp may parse successfully but
fall outside a business rule.

13
00:00:35.302 --> 00:00:37.569
A file may have a name but not an
acceptable purpose.

14
00:00:37.649 --> 00:00:41.286
Boundary code should convert external data
into validated internal types before

15
00:00:41.366 --> 00:00:42.052
sensitive use.

16
00:00:42.132 --> 00:00:45.822
This keeps downstream services from
repeating low-level parsing checks and makes

17
00:00:45.902 --> 00:00:47.905
application invariants easier to preserve.

18
00:00:47.985 --> 00:00:51.358
In Go, this often means decoding
carefully, applying explicit validation,

19
00:00:51.438 --> 00:00:55.023
returning predictable errors, and passing
a narrow value to business logic only

20
00:00:55.103 --> 00:00:56.104
after checks succeed.

21
00:00:56.184 --> 00:00:59.769
For HTTP APIs, request size limits and
predictable malformed-data handling are

22
00:00:59.849 --> 00:01:00.376
important.

23
00:01:00.456 --> 00:01:03.883
JSON decoding should be configured
intentionally for the route's contract.

24
00:01:03.963 --> 00:01:07.336
Strictness may be appropriate when unknown
fields would create ambiguity.

25
00:01:07.416 --> 00:01:10.526
Validation should happen before database
writes, downstream calls,

26
00:01:10.606 --> 00:01:13.031
authorization decisions that depend on
request data,

27
00:01:13.111 --> 00:01:15.378
or work that could consume significant
resources.

28
00:01:15.458 --> 00:01:17.145
The goal is not only to block bad input.

29
00:01:17.225 --> 00:01:20.282
The goal is to make trusted code simpler,
safer, and easier to review.

30
00:01:20.362 --> 00:01:22.523
Good validation also has a maintenance
benefit.

31
00:01:22.603 --> 00:01:24.765
When a future developer reads the boundary
code,

32
00:01:24.845 --> 00:01:28.271
they should be able to see which
assumptions are guaranteed for the rest of the

33
00:01:28.351 --> 00:01:28.773
request.

34
00:01:28.853 --> 00:01:31.225
Clear validation errors, focused helper
functions,

35
00:01:31.305 --> 00:01:34.731
and tests for rejected cases keep those
assumptions from drifting as fields,

36
00:01:34.811 --> 00:01:36.340
routes, and integrations evolve.
